===Indian Persian literature===
{{main|Persian language in the Indian subcontinent}}
During the early Muslim period, Persian became the official language of the northern part of Indian subcontinent, used by most of the educated and the government. The language had, from its earliest days in the 11th century CE, been imported to the subcontinent by various culturally Persianised [[Central Asia]]n [[Turkic peoples|Turkic]] and [[Afghans|Afghan]] dynasties.<ref>Sigfried J. de Laet. [https://books.google.com/books?id=PvlthkbFU1UC&dq=persian+language+in+anatolia&pg=PA734 ''History of Humanity: From the seventh to the sixteenth century''] UNESCO, 1994. {{ISBN|9231028138}} p 734</ref>

== In different communities ==

=== Literature from North East India ===
{{main|Literature from North East India}}

{{Further|#Ancient Meitei literature|#Assamese literature|#Modern Meitei literature}}
[[Literature from North East India]] included [[Assamese literature]], [[Meitei literature|Meitei or Manipuri literature]], Naga literature, among others. Ancient India has many intensive examples, like that of the incredible verses translated from the [[Ramayana]], named ''Saptakanda Ramayana''. Choral songs known as [[Oja-Pali]],<ref>{{Cite web|title=Ojapali – A performing art form of Assam|url=https://www.oknortheast.com/2012/03/ojapali-performing-art-form-of-assam.html?m=1|access-date=2021-01-22|website=oknortheast.com}}</ref> and theater performances, known as ''Panchali'', were also an extensive part of Assamese literature.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Barua|first=Hem|date=1957|title=Assamese literature|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/23328613|journal=Indian Literature|volume=1|issue=1|pages=65–68|jstor=23328613 |issn=0019-5804}}</ref>

=== Dalit literature ===
{{Main|Dalit literature}}
[[Dalit literature]] includes works written by [[Dalit|Dalits]] that explain their experiences and advocate for justice and equality. It expanded during the Dalit movement. The movement's activism and new artistic works advocate for caste equality and protest [[Caste system in India|caste]]-based oppression and [[Caste-related violence in India|violence]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Naskar |first=Shubhendu Shekhar |title=Aesthetics of Dalit Theatre |publisher=[[Palgrave Macmillan]] |year=2025 |chapter=History and Aesthetics of Dalit Literature |doi=10.1007/978-3-031-83453-0_4}}</ref>

=== LGBTQ literature ===
{{Main|LGBTQ literature in India}}
[[LGBTQ literature in India|Indian LGBTQ literature]], or [[LGBTQ literature|queer literature]], spans [[LGBTQ themes in Hindu mythology|mythological]] and ancient tales, works like "[[Lihaaf]]" published during periods of societal stigma, and recent writing as publishing becomes more accessible to the genre. These works may be about the [[LGBTQ people|LGBTQ]] community, or be written by [[List of LGBTQ writers|LGBTQ writers]], or address [[Outline of LGBTQ topics|themes]] relevant to LGBTQ people.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Chauhan |first=Saurabh |date=9 February 2019 |title=Beyond ‘Lihaaf’: Queer literature out of closet now |url=https://www.hindustantimes.com/lucknow/beyond-lihaaf-queer-literature-out-of-closet-now/story-fS7MrbKVl4fHFAZiwA0HtO.html |access-date=2026-04-14 |website=[[Hindustan Times]] |language=en}}</ref>

=== Prison literature ===
{{Main|Indian prison literature}}
[[Prison literature]] in [[Indian prison literature|India]] involves writing by authors who have been [[incarcerated]], and sometimes works written while they are incarcerated. The genre is predominantly made up of memoirs, autobiographies, and personal artifacts like letters and diaries. Often, these works provide political messaging that counters [[Dominant narrative|dominant narratives]] promoted by [[Institution|institutions]] of power. These works also can provide testimony about how [[incarcerated people]] are treated.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Kaveti |first=Bhargavi |date=April-June 2019 |title=The Plight of Subaltern Indian Women Prisoners- A Study of Mary Tyler's Autobiography "My Years in Indian Prison" |url=https://doi.org/10.33329/rjelal.7219.89 |journal=Research Journal of English Language and Literature |volume=7 |issue=2 |pages=89}}</ref> 

==Awards==
In contemporary Indian literature, there are two major literary awards; these are the [[List of Sahitya Akademi fellows|Sahitya Akademi Fellowship]] and the [[Jnanpith Award]]. Eight Jnanpith Awards each have been awarded in Hindi and [[Kannada language|Kannada]], followed by five in [[Bengali language|Bengali]] and [[Malayalam language|Malayalam]], four in [[Odia language|Odia]], [[Gujarati language|Gujarati]], [[Marathi language|Marathi]], [[Telugu language|Telugu]] and [[Urdu language|Urdu]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://jnanpith.net/|title=Jnanpith {{!}} Home|website=jnanpith.net|access-date=2019-07-31}}</ref><ref name=award2008>{{cite news|url=http://www1.timesofindia.indiatimes.com/Delhi/Kunwar_Narayan_to_be_awarded_Jnanpith/articleshow/3752703.cms|title=Kunwar Narayan to be awarded Jnanpith|date=24 November 2008|newspaper=The Times of India|access-date=2008-11-25|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://archive.today/20121205234801/http://www1.timesofindia.indiatimes.com/Delhi/Kunwar_Narayan_to_be_awarded_Jnanpith/articleshow/3752703.cms|archive-date=5 December 2012}}</ref> two each in [[Assamese language|Assamese]], [[Konkani language|Konkani]] and [[Tamil language|Tamil]], and one each in [[Sanskrit]] and Kashmiri۔{{citation needed|date=November 2023}}{{verify source|date=November 2023}}

